Dulcamara: a new sound channel is made while the mixer waits; a sound is opened before the mixer is made to wait.

check_channel grows the channels (realloc) as a game first uses one, a movie's or its mask's among them, without
the audio lock, while the mixer (callback) goes through them on its own thread: when realloc moves them, the mixer
reads freed memory. Dulcamara's own host tests crashed on it as they made many channels at once while sound played.
The growth now takes the audio lock (LOCK_AUDIO, SDL's: recursive).

RPS_play opened its new sample (load_stream: media_open, which joins the decode threads that have ended, and
media_start, which starts the sample's own) with the audio lock held, while RPS_queue opens its sample first and takes
the lock only to put it in place. Dulcamara's frontend tops its sound up from its main loop through the mixer, which
takes the same lock: as a game played a sound (a click on each menu choice), the frontend waited for the thread to
be started, and a console slow to start one (tests/core/slow_thread_start.c) held the lock, the frontend's sound
and its picture with it, for as long as that took: the game's music ran dry. RPS_play now opens the sample before it
takes the lock, as RPS_queue does; the old samples are still freed, and the new one put in place, with it held, and a
sample that does not open still leaves the channel stopped with Ren'Py's SOUND_ERROR.

Applied to Ren'Py 8's src/renpysound_core.c and Ren'Py 7's module/renpysound_core.c, the same there.

--- a/renpysound_core.c
+++ b/renpysound_core.c
